// ADR 0133 Phase 1 (BT-3902): a block may assign a captured variable only
// where the compiler lowers it in place, as the block literal written directly
// as the argument (or receiver) of a control construct or a compiler-known
// collection message. A block that escapes may read captured variables but
// not assign them; the compiler reports the assignment, naming how the block
// escapes. The REPL root reports the same diagnostic as a method (surface
// parity; BT-3858): each rejected input is refused before it runs, so no
// binding changes. This case folds in the former PIN-BUG cases
// adr0131_section6_escape_pins.btscript (BT-3806) and
// adr0132_section6_pins.btscript (BT-3866), whose shapes are now this error.
//
// @load tests/repl-protocol/fixtures/adr0132_section6_callee.bt

t := 0
// => 0

// ===========================================================================
// The inline shapes still work from the REPL.
// ===========================================================================

#(1, 2, 3) do: [:x | t := t + x]
// => _

t
// => 6

[t < 10] whileTrue: [t := t + 1]
// => nil

t
// => 10

[t := t + 1] value
// => 11

r := #(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:acc :x | t := t + 1. acc + x]
// => 3

t
// => 13

// Reads of a captured binding in an escaping block are fine.
reader := [t + 1]
// => Block/0

reader value
// => 14

// ===========================================================================
// Escaping shapes are compile errors, and leave `t` unchanged.
// ===========================================================================

t := 0
// => 0

// Passed to a user higher-order class method (ADR 0132's I1; I2 below).
Adr0132ReplCallee calleeDirect: [t := t + 1]
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block passed to

Adr0132ReplCallee calleeDo: [t := t + 1]
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block passed to

// Stored in a binding (ADR 0132's I6 stored it, then sent it to `do:`).
blk := [:x | t := t + x]
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block stored in `blk`

// The value of the input (BT-3858: it used to be displayed, and the first
// `value` raised).
[t := t + 1]
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block that is the value of the expression

// The value of a conditional arm, stored.
c := true ifTrue: [[t := t + 10]] ifFalse: [[0]]
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block stored in `c`

// An element of a collection literal.
d := #([t := t + 100])
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block placed in a collection literal

// None of the rejected inputs ran.
t
// => 0

// The fix the message names: the block answers the value; the caller assigns.
t := Adr0132ReplCallee calleeDirect: [t + 1]
// => 1

// ===========================================================================
// A method body reports the same diagnostic when its class is loaded: the
// actor self-send that ADR 0131 §6 exempted (BT-3755) is an escape.
// ===========================================================================

// @load-error tests/repl-protocol/fixtures/adr0133_escaping_block_error.bt => `t` is assigned in a block passed to `twice:`, which is not a construct the compiler threads it through
